  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nShortwave troughing draped across south-central Canada and the Upper \nMidwest will slide east today as a more subtle, convectively-\naugmented shortwave over the mid-Mississippi Valley also works east \nover the Ohio Valley. Combined forcing from these features will \nsupport elongated low pressure along a relatively weak cold front \nset to sweep across the region today. Surface high pressure will \ntemporarily build in behind this system on Monday before much more \nimpressive troughing rotates into the Great Lakes later Tuesday \nthrough the middle of the week. High pressure looks to build into \nthe Great Lakes again at the end of this week.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nMain focus of the forecast continues to be expected scattered \nshowers and storms across the area today. A few light showers may \npersist tonight/early this morning, but best chances will come late \nthis morning into early this evening along/ahead of the \naforementioned front. Best storm coverage is expected across the \neastern U.P. from late morning through early afternoon with \nconvection spreading into northern lower as time progresses. Ample \nbuoyancy (~1,000 J/kg MLCAPE) will be in place ahead of the front to \nsupport thunderstorms, albeit characterized by skinny-CAPE \nprofiles from the moisture-rich airmass present. This, combined \nwith limited deep-layer shear (~30 kts EBWD) will hinder the \npotential for strong thunderstorms today. The main concern with \nshowers and storms will be heavy rainfall potential with \naforementioned high-quality moisture in place. As noted by the \nprevious forecaster, PWAT values approaching 2\" will support \nefficient rainfall with any convection, bringing the potential \nfor localized totals of 0.5-1\"+ through this evening.\n\nRain chances will wind down this evening as high pressure builds in, \nkicking off the week with dry weather and sunshine. Chances for \nshowers and storms will re-enter the forecast Tuesday into Wednesday \nas previously mentioned troughing digs into the Great Lakes. Highs \nin the upper 70s to mid 80s during the start of the week will \nnoticeably cool during the middle of the week with afternoon \ntemperatures staying in the low to mid 70s for most areas.",
